142770,6R6q2fAp2n4,UgztDjT6QiMHaJHns0d4AaABAg,2023-04-07T10:36:33Z,6,0,"The intro is great. The film is a mixture of animation and realistic art. Suzume is a spunky, adolescent heroine with happy and dark childhood memories, on an adventurous journey while experiencing first love. It’s no small feat to have the hero turned into Suzume’s beloved keepsake, sometimes comical sounding like a galloping horse, sometimes a tragic hero. There’s a daijin cat with twists, a nagging but supportive aunt, a comical sidekick. An ever present villain resembles lava, posing disasters, and the heart wrenching 3/11 is included.  The OST is noteworthy from jazz to multi genre, from Tokyo to London/Prague. Brands like McDonalds, lichiko, Lawson and Band Aids add a familiar, realistic touch. It’s a film that speaks to Suzume’s past, hopeful present and a glimpse of her future.  I viewed this film twice and at each screening there was an audience member running out of and back in the theatre so as to not miss parts of Suzume. In addition, the Suzume team sent a 3D movie billboard of a door for audience photo ops. Thank you👍 Suzume opens in theatres April 11."
